1.1.text:
Part Three

Prevention and Protection

Article 22

(1) The Regional Government is obliged to undertake prevention and protection efforts against acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment, and neglect.

(2} The implementation of prevention and protection as referred to in paragraph (1), may cooperate with educational institutions, religious institutions, mass media, professional organizations, business entities, and/or community institutions.

(3) The prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (2) are implemented by:

a. establishing work networks;
b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of prevention and protection based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a system for prevention of violence and protection of children and women; d. disseminating Legislation related to the protection of women victims of violence; e. providing education for victims of violence and vulnerable groups;

JDIH Kota Singkawang

f. organizing family support in the form of family parenting education, child parenting education, family mediation, and economic support;

g. enhancing the capacity of protection services for women and children including institutional capacity development and improvement of professionalism and competence of service providers;

h. improving the ability of vulnerable individuals and/or groups to recognize risks and dangers from situations or acts that may cause violence, exploitation, maltreatment and neglect; and

I. increasing the utilization of communication, information, and education media.

(4) The prevention and protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the Regional Apparatus responsible for women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 66

(1) The targets of handling include:

a. children outside parental care;
b. children in disaster emergency situations;
c. children in conflict with the law; d. children victims of physical, mental and sexual violence;
e. children subject to maltreatment and neglect;
f. children victims of economic and sexual exploitation;
g. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse or matters that cause dependence on narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances;
h. children victims of pornography;
i. children with deviant social behavior;
j. children who experience stigmatization due to their parents' behavior; k. children and women who are victims of human trafficking;
l. children and women who are in situations where the nature and conditions of the workplace may endanger health, safety and morals; and
m. women victims of physical, mental and sexual violence. (2) Handling as referred to in paragraph (1) must be carried out immediately including:
a. identification process and receipt of complaints/reports; b. rescue action;
c. placement of children and women in temporary shelter homes; d. rehabilitation including:

JDIH Kota Singkawang

1) health recovery services;

2) psychosocial recovery services; and

3) legal assistance support;

e. social reintegration.

Article 2

(1) Every child has the right:

a. to live, grow, develop, and participate appropriately in accordance with human dignity and worth, and receive protection from criminal acts, violence, exploitation, and neglect;
b. to a name as personal identity and citizenship status; c. to worship according to their religion under parental guidance and practice their religious teachings; d. to know their parents, be raised, and cared for by their own parents; e. to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
f. to obtain education and teaching appropriate to age level, physical and mental condition, intelligence as well as interests and talents; g. to express and have their opinion heard as well as receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for self-development in accordance with moral values and propriety;
h. to rest and utilize leisure time, play, express themselves for self-development;
1. to receive proper care from parents/guardians or other parties responsible for their care;

JDIH Kota Singkawang

J. to obtain protection from abuse in politics, involvement in armed disputes, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of violence, involvement in war, being targets of persecution, torture or imposition of inhumane punishment and involvement of children in the worst forms of work;

k. to obtain protection from the dangers of narcotics, pornography, and violent shows or other things that impact changes in child growth and development;

I. to have contact with parents when separated; and

m. to obtain other rights in accordance with Laws and Regulations.

(2) Every woman has the right:

a. to live, sustain life, and improve their standard of living;
b. to live peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously physically and spiritually; c. to enjoy a good and healthy living environment; d. to live in a family within a legal marriage bond based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations; e. to obtain work according to ability, requirements and fair and decent wages;
f. specifically for women with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in public services;
g. to play an active role in politics and governance in accordance with their potential and capabilities;
h. to obtain information and legal services; and
i. to obtain other rights in accordance with their human dignity and based on the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
(3) Every victim has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal service; d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. assistance in accordance with applicable provisions;

JDIH Kota Singkawang

g. guarantee of rights related to the victim's status as a family member or community member; h. to obtain restitution in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and

i. other rights stipulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 98

Every person is prohibited from:

a. receiving child visitors and employing children in discotheque businesses, nightclubs, bars, karaoke, pubs/music houses, massage parlors and steam baths/saunas, and/or other worst places of work for children.
b. producing, selling and/or promoting cigarettes in the school environment.
c. selling and/or promoting cigarettes, gambling equipment, contraceptives and goods that can damage the morals of children.
d. broadcasting and displaying pornographic acts in community activities that can be accessed by children.
e. receiving child visitors during school hours or allowing child visitors after nine o'clock at night to access the internet at internet cafes.
f. refusing complaints and/or collecting fees in providing services to victims at Protection Service Institutions. g. treating children discriminatorily resulting in children experiencing material and moral losses thereby hindering their social function; h. treating children with disabilities discriminatorily; i. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of mistreatment and neglect;
J. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing violence against children;
k. committing violence or threat of violence forcing a child to have sexual intercourse with him/her or with another person; I. committing violence or threat of violence, coercing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading a child to commit or allow indecent acts to be committed; m. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking of children;

JDIH Kota Singkawang n. obstructing children from enjoying their own culture, recognizing and implementing the teachings of their religion and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;

o. recruiting or exploiting children for military and/or other purposes, and leaving children without protection of life;

