Article 64

(1) The provisions as referred to in Article 63 paragraph (1), may be excluded by taking into account the following requirements: a . the age for child workers in informal sector employment is above 15 (fifteen) years; b . obtaining written approval from the parent/guardian of the child worker in informal sector employment;

c. implementation must be set forth in a written employment agreement between the company/employer and the parent/guardian of the child worker in informal sector employment; d . not employed at night; e . working time of a maximum of 3 (three) hours per day;

JDIH Kota Singkawang

f. not employed in places or environments that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;

g. providing opportunities to socialize with family and the surrounding environment; h . providing opportunities to receive education in accordance with their talents and interests;

t. employed for light types of work; and

J. providing health protection for children and the opportunity for one day off per week.

(2) Protection for child workers in informal sector employment as referred to in paragraph (1), aims to: a . prevent all forms of exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence against children; and

b. protect children from activities that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health.

(3) Protection efforts for child workers in informal sector employment as referred to in paragraph (2), include: a . providing counseling to the community about children's rights; b. providing assistance in the form of psychological, medical and legal services to child workers in informal sector employment who experience exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence; c. empowering families through providing skills training and reducing expenditures; d . providing scholarships to child workers in informal sector employment who drop out of school to continue formal education to a higher level; and e . providing non-formal education and skills training for child workers in informal sector employment who do not pursue formal education.

